ty which was growing and  that feeling of unity is what is helping  us right now withstand this attack in  putin's speech to announce this attack  on ukraine he talked about how quote  ukraine is not just a neighboring  country  he said that it was quote an inalienable  part of russia's history culture and  spiritual space in that modern ukraine  was entirely created by russia  given this mindset  was there any chance  that ukraine would have a chance to  function as an autonomous  a truly autonomous state  yes and this is what ukrainians are  fighting for and this is why  myself all my parliamentary colleagues  the government the president are asking  of our western allies uh clearly what  putin has declared in his uh crazy mad  speech is a  is a pack of lies uh carefully drafted  for him by uh some  kremlin  masterminds who was an equally perverted  mind and a lack of knowledge about  history whatsoever so uh ukraine is an  independent country ukraine is not  russia  the ukrainian people love their freedom  we fight for our freedom uh we bring our  children up with the love for freedom  and uh this is what we're doing  essentially now we are fighting on the  defensive against an offensive from  russia  to have the right to exist and to live  and i think that um well any nation in  the world and any leader in the world  would be doing the same they would be  fighting for their country and the right  of their country to exist  in that same speech uh vladimir putin  also made the point that nato exercises  have been taking place in ukraine over  the past few years  and he said that military contingents of  nato countries have been almost  constantly present on ukrainian  territory under the pretext of exercises  given that nato has always been used as  an excuse by moscow  do you think that nato's involvement in  ukraine carrying out exercises  but falling short of giving ukraine  actual nato membership uh has it  ultimately hurt the country  look uh ukraine needs nato and nato  needs ukraine in order to stabilize  relations with russia uh that is as soon  as ukraine becomes a full-on uh member  of nato that's it putin has nothing else  to fight for the spears of influence as  he likes to call it uh would have been  decided  and uh  and over and again but uh  but  but respectfully the approach that's  been taken so far that is to say the  nato exercises uh the lack of full nato  membership  did it give  uh putin the pretext necessary to engage  in the actions we've seen over the last  36 hours  if it wasn't for nato putin would have  found another pretext  uh because putin wants ukraine inside of  russia and does not admit an independent  ukraine uh putin is crazy and this has  been proven time and time again starting  from his speech starting from the show  show a trial style uh way he made his  decisions to uh invade ukraine and have  recognition so to speak from his people  to invade ukraine uh  look uh we are dealing with a  psychopathic leader  of a very very big country of a very big  army and of a nuclear state and i think  that the world should really be mindful  of that  and not give in to his lies and his  manipulations and be aware that uh if we  are all to co-exist normally we should  follow the rules set up for us after  world war ii which work  pretty well i guess and when countries  follow them but when one country starts  falling out from those rules it's the uh  responsibility of the international  community  to stand up to it it says so in the u.n  charter which all these countries that  are now uh nato allies uh  they have signed it together with  ukraine so it's not the responsibility  of the international community to stand  up for ukraine because uh ukraine has  done nothing wrong we have made a free  choice as a sovereign state we have not  threatened russia in one way or the  other we have made a decision about how  we act with our own territory with our  own military and who are our partners  russia didn't like it so russia uses its  force and its might to invade ukraine  uh i think you can sort of sense here uh  the  who is in the wrong and who is in the  right like how can ukraine  a much much smaller country with a much  much smaller and less developed army  uh  be a threat  to i'm sorry i repeat myself but uh the  third biggest army in the world and the  biggest army in europe how  yeah we can we can agree that russia is  clearly aggressive uh last week  president zielinski appealed to the  international community as well as you  have just done saying we don't need your  sanctions after the bombardment will  happen and after our country will be  fired at or after we will have no  borders or after we will have no economy  why would we need those sanctions then  multiple countries multiple countries  have now announced that they will impose  unprecedented sanctions on russia  uh do you feel that this is ultimately  too little too late  what are these unprecedented sanctions  where are they i don't see them honestly  the unprecedented sanction would be the  swift why ukraine wants to switch russia  from the swift  swift operations are used with russia to  buy oil and gas oil and gas are the main  commodities resources which feed the  russian economy if they're switched off  from the swift it means that they don't  get the payments for the oil and gas so  immediately we sort of took two birds  with one stone there's also this uh you  know uh isolation of russian markets and  of russia's biggest markets  and uh  clearly this would be the sanction which  would hit the most just switching them  off from the swift and finding another  way to pay for their oil and gas well of  course they won't care about switching  up from the swift in that regard so it's  it has to come together  today uh i think that that the only  right and correct uh both morally and  legally thing to do on the united of the  international community is a provide  ukraine with enough defense capabilities  and actually get contingents here into  ukraine so that we can withstand further  blitzkrieg attacks from russia and two  is to impose such sanctions that would  actually isolate that country or rather  that crazy leader from the rest of the  world because the world does not need to  be poisoned with the toxicity of a  totalitarian regime  in an address to the nation on the first  day of the invasion president zielinski  said we have been left alone to defend  our state  who is ready to fight alongside us i  don't see anyone you've talked about the  kind of sanctions you'd like to see the  type of moves you'd like to see the  international community engage in is  there anything else you need the  international community to do to  recognize to engage in order to see uh  the current crisis in quickly  look the international community can  choose either to act or to continue with  just political statements political  statements are very nice we are very  grateful for them they show uh whose  side who  uh people are on but look when you do  tweet the hashtag stand with ukraine or  support ukraine it's not enough uh you  know it's not going to stop uh the  russian missiles and uh the russian  planes hovering over kiev and the  russian tanks uh moving into our roads  and destroying them completely along  with villages and along with innocent  civilians uh what needs to be done is  really show us uh if you're prepared to  stand with us because you're standing uh  with us not uh not just for the sake of  ukrainians and uh a pretty big country  in europe as well you're standing with  us for your own safety and security what  we need now is actually um  peacekeeping contingents in ukraine uh  since we are not nato members okay fine  fair enough  uh but uh u.n uh with the general  assembly can adopt a resolution on  sending peacekeeping contingents into  ukraine they don't need the security  council for that we can go the same way  that this was done um in korea in 191